## Common Electrical Design Mistakes to Avoid

Electrical design problems can lead to construction delays, equipment limitations, additional expenses, and operational challenges.

Careful planning and coordination can help reduce these risks.

### Underestimating Electrical Loads

If the design does not accurately reflect anticipated equipment demand, the building may require expensive upgrades later.

Load calculations should consider the proposed equipment schedule and applicable demand requirements.

### Overlooking Emergency Power Needs

Some buildings require designated equipment to operate during a power outage.

Emergency and standby power needs should be identified early, particularly where life safety, healthcare operations, or business continuity is involved.

### Poor Lighting Planning

Insufficient illumination, excessive glare, and poorly positioned fixtures can affect the usability of commercial spaces.

Lighting calculations and coordination with architectural layouts can help address these issues before installation.

### Ignoring Future Expansion

A design that meets only current requirements may become restrictive when the building expands or introduces new equipment.

Reasonable future allowances should be evaluated according to the project budget and anticipated operational needs.

### Inadequate MEP Coordination

Conflicts between electrical equipment, ducts, pipes, structural elements, and architectural features can cause field modifications.

Coordinated design reviews help identify these issues before construction progresses.

### Incomplete Documentation

Unclear drawings or missing specifications can lead to misunderstandings between engineers and contractors.

Construction documents should communicate the design intent and identify the relevant equipment, connections, and installation requirements.

### Delaying Engineering Involvement

Bringing electrical engineers into a project too late may limit design options and increase the likelihood of revisions.

Early involvement allows electrical requirements to be considered alongside the building layout and other engineering systems.

